\subsection{Inference Benchmark}
\label{app:benchmark}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{\textbf{Inference latency and memory} (MOMENT-small, A10G GPU, batch size 128). RR-MoA Top-2 adds $4.3$\,ms ($+10\%$) latency and $2.6$\,MB peak memory over a single adapter, negligible compared to the backbone forward pass.}
\label{tab:benchmark}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
Method & Latency (ms) & Peak GPU (MB) & Adapter params \\
\midrule
Backbone only & $44.3$ & $374$ & --- \\
Single adapter (conv) & $44.9$ & $375$ & $268$K \\
\textbf{RR-MoA (Top-2, $K{=}5$)} & $\mathbf{49.2}$ & $\mathbf{377}$ & $\mathbf{426}$\textbf{K} \\
\midrule
\rowcolor{gray!8} DLinear (no backbone) & $0.06$ & $173$ & $49$K \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The RR-MoA routing overhead ($+4.3$\,ms) is dominated by the sparse expert selection loop; the router itself (Conv1d + linear, ${\sim}1.1$K params) adds $<0.1$\,ms. At deployment scale, adapter swapping between tenants requires loading $426$K parameters (${\sim}1.7$\,MB) from host RAM, orders of magnitude cheaper than reloading a $35$M parameter backbone. At $N{=}100$ tenants, adapter-swapping throughput is $141$ tenants/sec. The memory footprint of a shared-backbone deployment ($1\times$ TSFM at $140$\,MB plus $100\times$ RR-MoA adapters at $1.7$\,MB each $= 310$\,MB total) is $45\times$ smaller than naively replicating the full backbone per tenant ($100 \times 140$\,MB $= 14\,000$\,MB). \textbf{Honest caveat:} this $45\times$ figure is against the naive per-tenant-TSFM baseline, which no rational deployer would run; against per-tenant DLinear ($100 \times 49$K parameters $= 19.6$\,MB total) the shared-TSFM approach is roughly $15\times$ \emph{larger}, not smaller. The memory argument for TSFMs over DLinear is therefore not the core motivation; that role is played by cross-task reuse, zero-shot onboarding, and the elimination of $O(N)$ per-tenant training pipelines (see \S\ref{sec:intro} and Appendix~\ref{app:deployment}).

\section{Extended Full Fine-Tuning Ablation}
\label{app:extended_ft}

To rule out optimization confounds in the Frozen Paradox, we extend full fine-tuning from the standard 15-epoch Adam protocol to stronger schedules: 50 epochs, cosine LR with 3-epoch warmup, layer-wise LR decay ($\gamma{=}0.8$), and AdamW with weight decay $0.01$.

\begin{table}[h]
